WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.362 --> 00:00:05.258
It’s no coincidence that Amazon’s biggest building is in India.

00:00:05.576 --> 00:00:09.180
In the southern city of Hyderabad, known as Asia’s Silicon Valley,

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:15.838
the tech giant’s campus covers 9.5 acres and holds 1.8 million square feet of office space.

00:00:15.838 --> 00:00:19.654
That’s 50 times the area of the Taj Mahal’s mausoleum.

00:00:19.845 --> 00:00:24.210
One of the first things you notice when you walk in are some country-specific themes.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:30.617
And plenty of quotes to motivate the 15,000 employees who work here, many of whom are engineers. 

00:00:30.617 --> 00:00:37.216
The building is jam-packed with workspaces for them. Even its headquarters in Seattle can’t hold that number of people.

00:00:37.216 --> 00:00:45.886
The company didn’t spare any expense - the cafeteria is open 24/7, there’s a cricket area, and even a landing pad for helicopters.

00:00:45.886 --> 00:00:51.807
Amazon’s choice of India for its mega-building is in line with Jeff Bezos’ obsession with long-term thinking.

00:00:51.807 --> 00:00:56.462
The e-commerce giant already dominates the online retail market in much of the West.

00:00:57.565 --> 00:01:04.358
But global dominance depends on cracking the Indian market with its 1.3 billion potential customers.

00:01:04.358 --> 00:01:05.012
Thank you. 

00:01:05.012 --> 00:01:12.891
It’s working hard to make inroads no matter the method - sometimes old technology is still the best option to make deliveries.

00:01:12.891 --> 00:01:15.574
But India has been a tough nut to crack.

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:20.694
In 2019, the government made it harder for multinational corporations to do business there.

00:01:20.694 --> 00:01:25.222
Amazon was banned from selling products from companies where it holds an equity stake.

00:01:25.222 --> 00:01:30.585
Items from the department store Shoppers Stop had to be removed since Amazon owns part of that chain.

00:01:30.585 --> 00:01:38.424
Analysts believe the new regulation was politically motivated - esigned to protect small businesses and keep shopowners happy enough

00:01:38.424 --> 00:01:40.312
 to re-elect prime minister Narendra Modi.

00:01:40.312 --> 00:01:44.259
Eventually, Shoppers Stop relisted items through a subsidiary.

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:51.630
But this just goes to show that it hasn’t been easy to operate in India, so you have to come up with ways to jump over the barriers.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:59.719
For Amazon, that has meant constructing a giant building to show the benefits you can bring to the local economy through the creation of new jobs.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:04.329
2,000 people worked on-site every day for over three years, according to the company. 

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:08.889
They used more than twice as much steel as the Eiffel Tower to build this beast.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:12.969
The only thing bigger is India’s growing appetite for the web.

00:02:12.969 --> 00:02:17.358
In 2016, about a quarter of the Indian population used the Internet.

00:02:17.358 --> 00:02:20.485
This year – over half are expected to be connected.

00:02:20.975 --> 00:02:24.974
The sharp increase is also attributed to the 4G rollout a couple of years ago.

00:02:24.974 --> 00:02:30.458
And if you add a rising middle class into this mix, this means more spending online.

00:02:30.458 --> 00:02:34.679
In 2010, the virtual retail market brought in less than a billion bucks.

00:02:34.679 --> 00:02:38.814
In 2024, it’s expected to top $100 billion.

00:02:39.059 --> 00:02:45.822
And there’s a lot of room to grow. E-commerce only represents about 4 percent of India’s total retail consumption.

00:02:45.822 --> 00:02:50.288
The pandemic appears to have further accelerated the trend toward online shopping.

00:02:50.288 --> 00:02:58.889
During Amazon’s deal event last year, Prime Day, it said twice as many customers in India signed up for a prime membership compared to the year before.

00:02:58.889 --> 00:03:02.864
Despite the growth, Amazon still faces some stiff competition - 

00:03:02.864 --> 00:03:08.469
especially from Walmart, which acquired the country’s largest online retailer, Flipkart, a few years ago.

00:03:08.469 --> 00:03:11.106
So, how do you go about beating your rival? 

00:03:11.106 --> 00:03:16.757
Bezos has always believed that it’s not about focusing on the competition but rather, the customer.

00:03:16.757 --> 00:03:21.998
This has meant appealing to the hundreds of millions of people who live in remote and rural areas.

00:03:21.998 --> 00:03:32.407
They often have to travel far to access a store or don’t have many options once there, so doorstep deliveries - by bike or by boat - would be life-changing.

00:03:32.407 --> 00:03:39.259
To help entice these customers to sign up, the Amazon app features descriptions in Hindi, works with cheap smartphones,

00:03:39.259 --> 00:03:42.176
and payments in cash at delivery is allowed. 

00:03:42.176 --> 00:03:49.466
Amazon is also trying to make its mark in the food and grocery industry because that comprises the largest part of India’s retail market.

00:03:49.466 --> 00:03:57.712
It launched a service similar to UberEats called Amazon Food last year - delivering prepared meals in select parts of Bangalore, another tech hub.

00:03:57.712 --> 00:04:01.357
It’s keen to grow Amazon Fresh - a grocery delivery service.

00:04:01.357 --> 00:04:09.761
And to further push into the food market, the tech giant partnered with a company operating major supermarket chains so their goods are sold on the site.

00:04:09.761 --> 00:04:17.249
Amazon hopes its foray into India is much more successful than what happened in China, where it failed to gain any traction  

00:04:17.249 --> 00:04:19.371
despite trying for more than a decade.

00:04:19.371 --> 00:04:25.698
It threw in the towel in 2019, when shoppers could no longer buy from local sellers on Amazon.cn.

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:30.072
The site is now limited to items available from the global store, like Kindles. 

00:04:30.072 --> 00:04:38.869
Amazon couldn’t compete against rivals Alibaba and JD.com that offered fast and often free shipping without any minimum order requirements.

00:04:38.869 --> 00:04:45.485
Amazon barely talks about its Chinese business but it’s certainly not shying away from discussing India.

00:04:45.485 --> 00:04:53.860
This massive building symbolizes its commitment to that nation - a nation where it's invested $6.5 billion since 2014.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:59.237
The pandemic has also shaped a new path for Amazon India as more people take up virtual learning.

00:04:59.237 --> 00:05:05.109
It recently launched an online academy to train students hoping to get into the nation’s top engineering schools.

00:05:05.109 --> 00:05:09.892
Becoming an engineer requires a solid base in math and computer science.

00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:14.595
To help you grasp these core subjects is a problem-solving website called Brilliant.

00:05:14.595 --> 00:05:15.903
It’s free to sign up.

00:05:15.903 --> 00:05:23.010
Whether you’re a student who doesn’t quite understand the Calculus material from class, or a professional hoping to brush up on your algorithms,

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:28.890
my sponsor Brilliant has over 60 interactive courses in math, science, and computer science.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.908
There are no tests, and you can learn at your own pace.

00:05:31.908 --> 00:05:34.696
You can sign up with this link, which is in my description.

00:05:34.696 --> 00:05:42.747
The first 200 people to use my link will get 20% off the Premium subscription, which gives you unlimited access to all courses.

